Dear Students,

Thank you to everyone who joined us at last Friday's meeting informing stud=
ents about the current instruction we're receiving from the University. To =
recap:

The Office of the Provost has asked all PhD programs for a more defined pla=
n for milestones/program timeframe due to several factors:

  *
Visa Changes: DHS's regulation limits international students to a 4-year vi=
sa, with students needing to manually apply for an extension in order to fi=
nish the PhD program. Defining these milestones will strengthen extension a=
pplications by pointing to a documented program timeline. While the DHS reg=
ulation is currently paused by a federal judge, the situation is changing d=
aily, and it is likely this will eventually move forward. Losing the abilit=
y to bring in international students is extremely harmful to the interdisci=
plinary, collaborative nature of our field and our program, and damaging to=
 your experience in addition to the College and University.
  *
Funding: The University is signaling that funding opportunities, such as TA=
ships, will no longer be available for students who extend past the dissert=
ation defense milestone (Year 6). In the past, this was more relaxed, but w=
e are hearing that this will no longer be the case. As a program, we've bee=
n lucky that our advisors have had money to help students navigate the last=
 part of the dissertation, but both PhD programs across NU and advisors ali=
ke are now dealing with tighter financial constraints.
  *
Travel Funding: As you know, the PhD Network Travel Funding has new eligibi=
lity restrictions this year - students may only apply if they have reached =
candidacy, and they may only be awarded funds once every other year. Helpin=
g you achieve candidacy on time will give you access to this funding suppor=
t.

Please remember that right now, the University is not enforcing the deadlin=
es for milestones. However, they may begin to do so as early as next year, =
so we want to strategize with you now and provide you with the full support=
 you need to move forward with confidence.

As a reminder, here are our program milestones:

  *
Complete coursework by Year 2
  *
Qualifying Exams in fall of Year 3
  *
Dissertation Proposal/Comprehensive Exam/Achieving Candidacy by end of Year=
 3
  *
Dissertation Defense by end of Year 6

For students in Year 3 and up who have not yet proposed, please do not let =
these milestones alarm you. As mentioned, the University is not enforcing t=
hese deadlines yet. But, it's in your best interest to propose soon; we sug=
gest the following to help you stay on track:

  *
Currently in Year 3 - we ask you to propose during the fall semester of you=
r fourth year (Fall 2027)
  *
Currently in Year 4 - we strongly advise you to propose as soon as possible=
, ideally by the end of this year (Summer 2027)
  *
Currently Year 5 and up - we strongly advise you to propose by the end of t=
his year (Summer 2027)

Important Notes:

  *
If you have any concerns, please come talk to us. Your advisor, Esteban, an=
d I will work with you to help come up with a plan and timeline that is bes=
t for you and your progress in the program. Your advisors have been informe=
d about this situation and we will bring this up at the next faculty meetin=
g.
  *
This issue is not limited to just our program or just within Northeastern. =
This is an issue that higher education institutions nationwide are navigati=
ng. As a program, we are advocating for you and pushing back however we can=
; remember that you, as a student, can also make your voice heard.
  *
Remember that YOU drive your progress and ability to meet these milestones.=
 It is up to you to take the initiative to speak with your advisor and keep=
 moving forward. Please talk with your advisor about your proposal plans.
  *
Remember that you can use the new Dissertation & Graduation Deadlines Track=
er Tool<https://nam12.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=3Dhttps%3A%2F%2=
Fwww.networkscienceinstitute.org%2Fphd-program-deadline-tracker&data=3D05%7=
C02%7Clevine.ch%40northeastern.edu%7C8184c6d78f59491fed0e08df257a56f9%7Ca8e=
ec281aaa34daeac9b9a398b9215e7%7C0%7C0%7C639270879983467137%7CUnknown%7CTWFp=
bGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjo=
iTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&sdata=3DANljOdBHF1FVv0V7cgxQdDTg6ee%=
2BqaASsw0GTpfUKvQ%3D&reserved=3D0> to help with your planning.


Dissertation Proposal Guidance
We are not changing the format of the dissertation proposal. Instead, we ar=
e changing how we have been treating the proposal.

Traditionally, our program has been unique, treating the proposal like a "r=
ehearsal for the dissertation defense"--a presentation of your research tha=
t is already well under way and held not too far in advance of the disserta=
tion defense. In reality, the proposal establishes what your dissertation w=
ill be, why it matters, and whether the plan is feasible. It is simply that=
 - a plan. There is no formal requirement that you have papers published or=
 that you have completed work to present. Please refer to the attached prop=
osal guide and template for more defined guidance. The proposal format and =
timing should be determined in conversation with your advisor, so talk to t=
hem about their expectations and your current research progress.



If you have any questions or concerns, please come talk to me, Esteban, or =
your advisor. If you are nearing one of the program milestones above, you a=
nd your advisor will receive a specific email from me in the next week to c=
heck in.

Thank you,
Melissa


